)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139539079 17592186576411 true] [17592186069397 1071 "Evaluate $\\int \\frac{2}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139539079 17592186576411 false] [17592186069397 1074 "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+1$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{A}{x-1}+\\frac{Bx+C}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-1)({x}^{2}+1),$ and we get\n\n$$\n2=A({x}^{2}+1)+(Bx+C)(x-1).\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=1,B=-1,$ and $C=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{-x-1}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{-x-1}{{x}^{2}+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|.\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+1.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+1| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right),\n$$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{(x-1)({x}