second equals the third, the first must equal the third.\n\nWe can rewrite the given sentence as follows:\n\n> If $2 \\cdot 5 = 10$ and $10 = 4+6$, then $2 \\cdot 5 = 4+6$.\n\nTherefore, this statement is true by the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{transitive}}$ property of equality." 13194139539079 17592186576411 true] [17592186073511 1074 "The following properties are valid for all integers $a, b$, and $c{:}$\n\n- The *reflexive* property of equality:\n$a = a$\nThis states that every number equals itself.\n- The *symmetric* property of equality:\n$\\textrm{If}\\: a = b, \\textrm{then}\\: b=a$\nThis states that if the first number equals the second, the second must equal the first.\n- The *transitive* property of equality:\n$\\textrm{If}\\: a = b \\:\\textrm{and}\\: b=c,\\textrm{then}\\:a=c$\nThis states that if the first number equals the second and the second equals the third, the first must equal the third.\n\nWe can rewrite the given sentence as follows:\n\n> If $2 \\cdot 5 = 10$ and $10 = 4+6$, then $2 \\cdot 5 = 4+6$.\n\nTherefore, this statement is true by the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{transitive}}$ property of equality." 13194139539079 17592186576411 false] [17592186073516 1074 "![](images/00/008b75ac2feaec944a50368ec0fc5781d47314f8bfe0503ae70c1e564b4035b1.png)\n\nThe correct proof is below.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Given}}$ |\n| 2 | $\\angle{BCD} \\cong \\angle{CBE}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Alternate interior angles theorem}}$ |\n| 3 | $m\\angle{BCD} = m\\angle{CBE}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent angles}}$ |\n| 4 | $m\\angle{CBE} = m\\angle{ABE} + m\\angle{ABC}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Angle addition postulate}}$ |\n| 5 | $\\angle{ABE} \\cong \\angle{A}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Alternate interior angles theorem}}$ |\n| 6 | $m\\angle{ABE} = m\\angle{A}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent angles}}$ |\n| 7 | $m\\angle{CBE} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ABC}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Substitution}}$ |\n| 8 | $m\\angle{BCD} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ABC}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Transitive property of equality}}$ |\n\nLet's examine each row in turn.\n\n1. Note that we're given $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD}$.\n2. From row 1, we have that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD}$ are parallel. Therefore, $\\angle{BCD}$ and $\\angle{CBE}$ are congruent by the alternate interior angles theorem.\n3. From row 2, we have that $\\angle{BCD}$ and $\\angle{CBE}$ are congruent. Therefore, by the definition of congruent angles, $m\\angle{BCD} = m\\angle{CBE}$.\n4. Note that the segment $\\overline{AB}$ divides the angle $\\angle{CBE}$ into consecutive angles $\\angle{ABE}$ and $\\angle{ABC}$. Therefore, by the angle addition postulate, we must have\n$m\\angle{CBE} = m\\angle{ABE} + m\\angle{ABC}$.\n5. From row 1, we have that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD}$ are parallel. Therefore, $\\angle{ABE}$ and $\\angle{A}$ are congruent by the alternate interior angles theorem.\n6. From row 5, $\\angle{ABE}$ and $\\angle{A}$ are congruent. Therefore, by the definition of congruent angles, $m\\angle{ABE} = m\\angle{A}$.\n7. From row 6, $m\\angle{ABE} = m\\angle{A}$. Substituting this into the equation in row 4, we have\n$m\\angle{CBE} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ABC}$.\n8. From rows 3 and 7, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle m\\angle{BCD} = m\\angle{CBE}, \\qquad m\\angle{CBE} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ABC}.\n$$\nTherefore, by the transitive property of equality, $m\\angle{BCD} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ABC}$." 13194139539079 17592186576411 true] [17592186073516 1074 "![](images/00/008b75ac2feaec944a50368ec0fc5781d47314f8bfe0503ae70c1e564b4035b1.png)\n\nThe correct proof is below.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Given}}$ |\n| 2 | $\\angle{BCD} \\cong \\angle{CBE}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Alternate interior angles theorem}}$ |\n| 3 | $m\\angle{BCD} = m\\angle{CBE}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent angles}}$ |\n| 4 | $m\\angle{CBE} = m\\angle{ABE} + m\\angle{ABC}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Angle addition postulate}}$ |\n| 5 | $\\angle{ABE} \\cong \\angle{A}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Alternate interior angles theorem}}$ |\n| 6 | $m\\angle{ABE} = m\\angle{A}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent angles}}$ |\n| 7 | $m\\angle{CBE} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ABC}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Substitution}}$ |\n| 8 | $m\\angle{BCD} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ABC}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Transitive property of equality}}$ |\n\nLet's examine each row in turn.\n\n1. Note that we're given $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD}$.\n2. From row 1, we have that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD}$ are parallel. Therefore, $\\angle{BCD}$ and $\\angle{CBE}$ are congruent by the alternate interior angles theorem.\n3. From row 2, we have that $\\angle{BCD}$ and $\\angle{CBE}$ are congruent. Therefore, by the definition of congruent angles, $m\\angle{BCD} = m\\angle{CBE}$.\n4. Note that the segment $\\overline{AB}$ divides the angle $\\angle{CBE}$ into consecutive angles $\\angle{ABE}$ and $\\angle{ABC}$. Therefore, by the angle addition postulate, we must have\n$m\\angle{CBE} = m\\angle{ABE} + m\\angle{ABC}$.\n5. From row 1, we have that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD}$ are parallel. Therefore, $\\angle{ABE}$ and $\\angle{A}$ are congruent by the alternate interior angles theorem.\n6. From row 5, $\\angle{ABE}$ and $\\angle{A}$ are congruent. Therefore, by the definition of congruent angles, $m\\angle{ABE} = m\\angle{A}$.\n7. From row 6, $m\\angle{ABE} = m\\angle{A}$. Substituting this into the equation in row 4, we have\n$m\\angle{CBE} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ABC}$.\n8. From rows 3 and 7, we have\n$m\\angle{BCD} = m\\angle{CBE}, \\qquad m\\angle{CBE} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ABC}$.\nTherefore, by the transitive property of equality, $m\\angle{BCD} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ABC}$." 13194139539079 17592186576411 false] [17592186073544 1071 "![](images/e5/e52880d8db4da89f583cd3fe5323b7f7c551b5a4e2a5b657aeed7177ec367827.png)\n\nThe equation below gives the area of the shaded region. From *left to right*, what numbers are missing?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{Area} = \\dfrac{\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}}{3} \\times \\dfrac{3}{\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}} = \\dfrac{\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}}{12}\n$$" 13194139539079 17592186576411 true] [17592186073544 1071 "![](images/e5/e52880d8db4da89f583cd3fe5323b7f7c551b5a4e2a5b657aeed7177ec367827.png)\n\nThe equation below gives the area of the shaded region. From *left to right*, what numbers are missing?\n\n$\\textrm{Area} = \\dfrac{\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}}{3} \\times \\dfrac{3}{\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}} = \\dfrac{\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}}{12}$" 13194139539079 17592186576411 false] [17592186073544 1074 "First, notice the unit square is divided into $3$ equal slices horizontally and into $4$ slices vertically. So, each of the small tiles is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nunits long a