 13194139539079 17592186576411 false] [17592186073598 1074 "Given two similar polygons, the similarity ratio is the ratio of any two corresponding sides. \n\nNotice that, for example, $\\overline{LM}$ is the longest side of $NLMP.$ Hence, it corresponds to the longest side of $ABCD,$ which is  $\\overline{BC}.$ \n\nTherefore, the similarity ratio is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{BC}{LM} \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{22} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The ratio of the smaller polygon to the larger polygon would be $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)}=\\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$" 13194139539079 17592186576411 true] [17592186073598 1074 "Given two similar polygons, the similarity ratio is the ratio of any two corresponding sides. \n\nNotice that, for example, $\\overline{LM}$ is the longest side of $NLMP.$ Hence, it corresponds to the longest side of $ABCD,$ which is  $\\overline{BC}.$ \n\nTherefore, the similarity ratio is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{BC}{LM} \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{22} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The ratio of the smaller polygon to the larger polygon would be $\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)}=\\dfrac{2}{3}.$" 13194139539079 17592186576411 false] [17592186073608 1074 "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The measure of the minor arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ is equal to the measure of its central angle,\n$$\n\\displaystyle m\\overset{\\frown}{AB} = m\\angle{AOB}=120^\\circ.\n$$\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. The arc $\\overset{\\frown}{ACB}$ represents the major arc of the circle, so it has the measure\n$$\n\\displaystyle m\\overset{\\frown}{ACB} = 360^\\circ - m\\angle{AOB} =360^\\circ - 120^\\circ =240^\\circ.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." 13194139539079 17592186576411 true] [17592186073608 1074 "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The measure of the minor arc $\\overset{\\fro