3$ implies\n\n$$\nAC:CB = 1:3\n$$\n\nThis means\n\n- there are $1+3 = 4$ equal parts, and\n- we want to find the point $C$ that is $1$ equal part from $A$. So, we have\n$AC= \\dfrac{1}{4}AB$.\n\nNext, consider another point $X$ that is horizontal with $A$ and directly below $C$, as shown in the diagram.\n\n![](images/29/29b01b5cc8cca113a14c16f95626faadb70993bbb42fab7fd77f011ecf0de734.png)\n\nTo get from point $A$ to point $C$, we can \"run\" horizontally from $A$ to $X$ and then \"rise\" vertically from $X$ to $C$:\n\n- The $x$-coordinate of $C$ is\n$$\n\\displaystyle x_{C}|= x_{A} + \\frac{1}{4}(x_{B} - x_{A}); = 2 + \\frac{1}{4}(6 - 2); = 2 + 1; = 3.\n$$\n- The $y$-coordinate of $C$ is\n$$\n\\displaystyle y_{C}|= y_{A} + \\frac{1}{4}(y_{B} - y_{A}); = 1 + \\frac{1}{4}(9 - 1); = 1 + 2; = 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $C$ has coordinates\n\n$$\n\\left(3, 3 \\right)\n$$" 13194139539079 17592186576411 true] [17592186073652 1074 "Partitioning a line segment $\\overline{AB}$ into a ratio $a:b$ means we must\n\n- divide the segment into $a+b$ equal parts, and\n- find the point $C$ that is $a$ equal parts from $A$.\n\nIn our case, the ratio $1:3$ implies\n\n$$\nAC:CB = 1:3\n$$\n\nThis means\n\n- there are $1+3 = 4$ equal parts, and\n- we want to find the point $C$ that is $1$ equal part from $A$. So, we have\n$AC= \\dfrac{1}{4}AB$.\n\nNext, consider another point $X$ that is horizontal with $A$ and directly below $C$, as shown in the diagram.\n\n![](images/29/29b01b5cc8cca113a14c16f95626faadb70993bbb42fab7fd77f011ecf0de734.png)\n\nTo get from point $A$ to point $C$, we can \"run\" horizontally from $A$ to $X$ and then \"rise\" vertically from $X$ to $C$:\n\n- The $x$-coordinate of $C$ is\n$x_{C}|= x_{A} + \\frac{1}{4}(x_{B} - x_{A}); = 2 + \\frac{1}{4}(6 - 2); = 2 + 1; = 3$.\n- The $y$-coordinate of $C$ is\n$y_{C}|= y_{A} + \\frac{1}{4}(y_{B} - y_{A}); = 1 + \\frac{1}{4}(9 - 1); = 1 + 2; = 3$.\n\nTherefore, $C$ has coordinates\n\n$$\n\\left(3, 3 \\right)\n$$" 13194139539079 175921