rac{{2}^{k}}{4}| & <1 \\\\ \\frac{{2}^{k}}{4} & <1 \\\\ {2}^{k} & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because $\\dfrac{2^k}{4}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation $2^k = 4$ has the solution $k=2.$ Now, since $2^k$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to $2^k < 4$ must be $k < 2.$" 13194139539079 17592186576411 false] [17592186078344 1071 "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow 1^+}\\dfrac{\\mid x-1 \\mid}{\\sqrt{x-1}}.\n$$" 13194139539079 17592186576411 true] [17592186078344 1071 "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow 1^+}\\dfrac{\\mid x-1 \\mid}{\\sqrt{x-1}}$." 13194139539079 17592186576411 false] [17592186078346 1071 "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0^-} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x^2}}{x}.\n$$" 13194139539079 17592186576411 true] [17592186078346 1071 "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0^-} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x^2}}{x}$." 13194139539079 17592186576411 false] [17592186078348 1071 "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 1}^ \\infty} 4 (-3)^{n}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 6}^\\infty} 32 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{4n}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}} \\dfrac{4^n}{2^{n+3}}\n$$" 13194139539079 17592186576411 true] [17592186078348 1071 "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n- $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 1}^ \\infty} 4 (-3)^{n}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 6}^\\infty} 32 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{4n}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}} \\dfrac{4^n}{2^{n+3}}$" 13194139539079 17592186576411 false] [17592186078348 1074 "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- The series $$\n\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 1}^ \\infty} 4 (-3)^{n}\n$$ starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{4(-3)^{2}}{4(-3)^{1}} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r| \\geq 1.$\n\n- The series $$\n\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 6}^\\infty} 32 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{4n}\n$$ starts with $n=6.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{7}}{{a}_{6}} & =\\frac{32{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{4(7)}}{32{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{4(6)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{28}}{32{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{24}} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r| < 1.$\n\n- The series $$\n\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}} \\dfrac{4^n}{2^{n+3}}\n$$ starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{1+3}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{{4}^{0}}{{2}^{0+3}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{4}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{{4}^{0}}{{2}^{3}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{4}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{4}^{0}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r| \\geq 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series II converges." 13194139539079 17592186576411 true] [17592186078348 1074 "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- The series $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 1}^ \\infty} 4 (-3)^{n}$ starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{4(-3)^{2}}{4(-3)^{1}} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r| \\geq 1.$\n\n- The series $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 6}^\\infty} 32 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{4n}$ starts with $n=6.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{7}}{{a}_{6}} & =\\frac{32{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{4(7)}}{32{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{4(6)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{28}}{32{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{24}} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r| < 1.$\n\n- The series $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}} \\dfrac{4^n}{2^{n+3}}$ starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{1+3}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{{4}^{0}}{{2}^{0+3}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{4}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{{4}^{0}}{{2}^{3}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{4}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{4}^{0}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r| \\geq 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series II converges." 13194139539079 17592186576411 false] [17592186078349 1071 "Determine $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2^-} \\dfrac {\\vert x^2 - 4 \\vert} {x - 2}.\n$$" 13194139539079 17592186576411 true] [17592186078349 1071 "Determine $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2^-} \\dfrac {\\vert x^2 - 4 \\vert} {x - 2}$." 13194139539079 17592186576411 false] [17592186078358 1071 "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x - 2\\sqrt{x}} {x - 4} }.\n$$" 13194139539079 17592186576411 true] [17592186078358 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x - 2\\sqrt{x}} {x - 4} }.$" 13194139539079 17592186576411 false] [17592186078359 1071 "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac {3x} {\\sqrt{x +1} -1}  }.\n$$" 13194139539079 17592186576411 true] [17592186078359 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac {3x} {\\sqrt{x +1} -1}  }.$" 13194139539079 17592186576411 false] [17592186078371 1071 "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0}f(x)\n$$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted below.\n\n![](images/dc/dcee0bc0465b5cd26f982d11ea7de02c477c4215e4cbdb9b463241ed225b8850.png)" 13194139539079 17592186576411 true] [17592186078371 1071 "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0}f(x)$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted below.\n\n![](images/dc/dcee0bc0465b5cd26f982d11ea7de02c477c4215e4cbdb9b463241ed225b8850.png)" 13194139539079 17592186576411 false] [17592186078373 1071 "![](images/94/94ff7096b1befc0ce2538ef