 =8 \\\\ LN & =\\frac{8}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({37}^{∘})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LN & =\\frac{8}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({37}^{∘})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{(0.753\\,55…)} \\\\  & =10.616\\,35… \\\\  & ≈11,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139539079 17592186576411 true] [17592186078725 1074 "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the given triangle.\n\n![](images/38/38dcba98867c158c671b35ec033fa1ec55c8cedc67466ebe981de9fbbae314e9.png)\n\nNotice that we are given the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{LM}$ opposite to the given angle $∠N$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{LN}$ adjacent$∠N.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the opposite leg to the adjacent leg is *tangent*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠N) & =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({37}^{∘}) & =\\frac{8}{LN} \\\\ LN\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({37}^{∘}) & =8 \\\\ LN & =\\frac{8}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({37}^{∘})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LN & =\\frac{8}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({37}^{∘})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{(0.753\\,55…)} \\\\  & =10.616\\,35… \\\\  & ≈11,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139539079 17592186576411 false] [17592186078742 1071 "![](images/82/824f039d5f015a2cd2165cfd469c44e7f3e5a0cda6416964ec91d57de4ca06eb.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139539079 17592186576411 true] [17592