82926 1071 "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}^n\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 n & 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n\\quad\\text{for all }\\, n \\in \\mathbb{N}\n$$\n\nWhat are the missing entries in the proof template below?\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n$$\n\\boxed{ {\\phantom{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}}\n}\n=\n\\boxed{ {\\phantom{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}}\n}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n = \\boxed{ {\\phantom{000000}}},$ we have\n$$\n\\boxed{ {\\phantom{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}}\n}\n=\n\\boxed{ {\\phantom{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}}\n}\n.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n$$\n\\boxed{ {\\phantom{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}}\n}\n=\n\\boxed{ {\\phantom{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}}\n}\n.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}^n\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 n & 1\\end{array} \\right]}$\nfor all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}.$" 13194139539079 17592186576411 false] [17592186082926 1074 "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove the following:\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}^n\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 n & 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the **base case**, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our c