igned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(4x^2-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-3 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x= -\\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}, \\; \\dfrac12, \\; \\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}.$" 13194139539079 17592186576411 false] [17592186083045 1071 "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{100} n.\n$$" 13194139539079 17592186576411 true] [17592186083045 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{100} n$." 13194139539079 17592186576411 false] [17592186083046 1071 "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{100} n^2.\n$$" 13194139539079 17592186576411 true] [17592186083046 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{100} n^2$." 13194139539079 17592186576411 false] [17592186083047 1071 "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{100} 4n.\n$$" 13194139539079 17592186576411 true] [17592186083047 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{100} 4n$." 13194139539079 17592186576411 false] [17592186083048 1071 "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^{100} 4n^2.\n$$" 13194139539079 17592186576411 true] [17592186083048 1071 "Calculate\n$\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^{100} 4n^2$." 13194139539079 17592186576411 false] [17592186083049 1071 "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{200} (3n+2).\n$$" 13194139539079 17592186576411 true] [17592186083049 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{200} (3n+2)$." 13194139539079 17592186576411 false] [17592186083049 1074 "We can write the summation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(200)(3n + 2) &= ∑_(n = 1)^(200)3n + ∑_(n = 1)^(200)2 \\\\\n&= 3∑_(n = 1)^(200)n + 2∑_(n = 1)^(200)1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the two series separately.\n\n- For the first series, we use the formula\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^N n = \\dfrac 1 2 N (N+1)\n$$\nwith $N=200$, and we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle 3∑_(n = 1)^(200)n|= 3(\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 200 \\cdot 201); = 3 \\cdot 20100; = 60300.\n$$\n- In the second series, $$\n\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=1}^{200} 1}\n$$ is just $1$ added together $200$ times, which is $200 \\cdot 1 = 200$. So, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle 2\\sum_{n=1}^{200} 1 =2 \\cdot 200 = 400.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^{200} (3n+2) = 60\\,300 + 400 = 60\\,700\n$$" 13194139539079 17592186576411 true] [17592186083049 1074 "We can write the summation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(200)(3n + 2) &= ∑_(n = 1)^(200)3n + ∑_(n = 1)^(200)2 \\\\\n&= 3∑_(n = 1)^(200)n + 2∑_(n = 1)^(200)1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the two series separately.\n\n- For the first series, we use the formula\n$\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^N n = \\dfrac 1 2 N (N+1)$\nwith $N=200$, and we get\n$3∑_(n = 1)^(200)n|= 3(\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 200 \\cdot 201); = 3 \\cdot 20100; = 60300$.\n- In the second series, $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=1}^{200} 1}$ is just $1$ added together $200$ times, which is $200 \\cdot 1 = 200$. So, we have\n$2\\sum_{n=1}^{200} 1 =2 \\cdot 200 = 400$.\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^{200} (3n+2) = 60\\,300 + 400 = 60\\,700\n$$" 13194139539079 17592186576411 false] [17592186083050 1071 "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^{50} (3n^2+2n).\n$$" 13194139539079 17592186576411 true] [17592186083050 1071 "Calculate\n$\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^{50} (3n^2+2n)$." 13194139539079 17592186576411 false] [17592186083050 1074 "Notice that we can write the given summation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(50)(3n^{2} + 2n) &= ∑_(n = 1)^(50)3n^{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(50)2n \\\\\n&= 3∑_(n = 1)^(50)n^{2} + 2∑_(n = 1)^(50)n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate the two series using our formulas.\n\n- For the first series, we use the formula\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^N n^2 = \\dfrac1 6 N (N+1)(2N+1)\n$$\nwith $N=50$, and we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle 3∑_(n = 1)^(50)n^{2}|= 3(\\frac{1}{6} \\cdot 50(50 + 1)(2 \\cdot 50 + 1)); = 3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot 50 \\cdot 51 \\cdot 101; = 3 \\cdot 42925; = 128775.\n$