ivalent to $\\cos{n} \\geq -1$. This inequality is true for all $n \\geq 1$.\n- The inequality $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\cos{n} +2}{\\sqrt{n}} \\geq \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$ is equivalent to $\\cos{n} \\geq 0$. This inequality is *not* true for all $n\\geq 1$.\n- The sequence $b_n = \\dfrac{2}{n^2}$ is not suitable since the series $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{2}{n^2}\n$$ is convergent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139539079 17592186576411 true] [17592186083095 1074 "The comparison test for divergence states the following:\n\n> *Consider two series $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$ and $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n$ such that $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive and $a_n \\geq b_n$ for all $n$. If $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n$ is divergent, then $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$ is also divergent.*\n\nFor the test to be valid, we require that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\nis divergent, and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos{n} +3}{\\sqrt{n}} \\geq b_n\n$$\n\nfor all\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nLet's look at each potential sequence in turn.\n\n- The inequality $\\dfrac{\\cos{n} +2}{\\sqrt{n}} \\geq \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}$ is equivalent to $\\cos{n} \\geq -1$. This inequality is true for all $n \\geq 1$.\n- The inequality $\\dfrac{\\cos{n} +2}{\\sqrt{n}} \\geq \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{n}}$ is equivalent to $\\cos{n} \\geq 0$. This inequality is *not* true for all $n\\geq 1$.\n- The sequence $b_n = \\dfrac{2}{n^2}$ is not suitable since the series $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{2}{n^2}$ is convergent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139539079 17592186576411 false] [17592186083096 1071 "Use the alternating series test to show that the series $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\frac{(-1)^{n+1}\\,n}{n^2+1}\n$$ is convergent." 13194139539079 17592186576411 true] [17592186083096 1071 "Use the alternating series test to show that the series $\\displaystyle \\su